1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,010
This video shows you how to archive your records to S3,

2
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:08,990
as Parquet files, and read one back.

3
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:13,769
Your records now go to Elasticsearch, where you search the recent ones.

4
00:00:13,769 --> 00:00:17,233
Keeping every record there for years can cost a lot.

5
00:00:17,233 --> 00:00:22,150
An archive in S3 keeps them all, cheaply, for as long as you need them.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,660
On the same route, add a second destination.

7
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:27,361
This time, S3.

8
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,495
Call it archive, and give it the bucket.

9
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,787
The prefix sets where files go.

10
00:00:33,787 --> 00:00:38,190
These date codes put each day's files in a folder of their own.

11
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,630
Write Parquet, a column format that query engines read quickly.

12
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:47,755
It signs in with the worker host's own AWS credentials, such as an instance role.

13
00:00:47,755 --> 00:00:49,550
There's nothing to store.

14
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,798
Under Endpoint and delivery, set the region.

15
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:57,667
This bucket is in an S3-compatible store, so give its endpoint too.

16
00:00:57,667 --> 00:01:00,230
On AWS, leave the endpoint empty.

17
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:05,670
smeltr finishes a file five minutes after its first record, or sooner when it's full.

18
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:07,196
Add it.

19
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:13,837
Put it on a branch called deliver-s3, save, and open it for review.

20
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:27,799
Approve and deploy it.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,159
Send a fresh record.

22
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:46,890
After the file interval, list the bucket.

23
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:52,779
Copy the file down, and show its rows.

24
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:57,670
There's your record, as a row of a Parquet file.

25
00:01:57,720 --> 00:01:59,532
Check on learning.

26
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:04,437
You sent a record, and no file appeared for minutes.

27
00:02:04,437 --> 00:02:05,399
Why?

28
00:02:09,799 --> 00:02:15,784
smeltr finishes a file five minutes after its first record, or sooner when it's full.

29
00:02:15,784 --> 00:02:19,623
Until then, the record waits in the open file.

30
00:02:20,523 --> 00:02:25,142
When the bucket is on AWS, what do you leave out?

31
00:02:29,542 --> 00:02:30,920
The endpoint.

32
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:36,076
Without one, smeltr writes to AWS's own S3.

33
00:02:36,976 --> 00:02:41,782
Next, you send your records as logs, over OpenTelemetry.
